    "accordion_X9KDBB": {
      "type": "accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "text_8HeDjV": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is Philadelphia tap water safe to drink for infants and vulnerable groups? ",
            "answer": "<p>Despite saying that Philadelphia tap water safety complies with legal standards, PFAS can be found in Philadelphia water and can leach away from aged pipes. This makes Philadelphia water not a healthy choice for Sensitive Groups. A suitable filter will make sure a maximum reduction of Philadelphia water contamination. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_QbTBtT": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why is the water quality report showing high levels of TTHMs? ",
            "answer": "<p>In the <strong>Philadelphia water quality report</strong> TTHMs are usually very high because Philadelphia uses a lot of chlorine in its water. Chlorine combined with substances in the Schuylkill and Delaware River creates TTHMs. Many health professionals think that TTHMs are too high for appropriate long-term consumption. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_XbcVQV": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is there a high level of arsenic in Philadelphia water? ",
            "answer": "<p>Arsenic in Philadelphia water is vigilantly tested. Though usually lower than the legal limit of 10 ppb, it is deemed unsafe by health advocates. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_NXHddK": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can a Phoenix Gravity filter remove PFAS in Philadelphia water?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Our filters are meant to eliminate the current Philadelphia drinking water challenges such as PFOA/PFOS removal. They are an effective way to block these \"forever chemicals\" that typical pitcher filters can't. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_yUeHba": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I know if my filter is handling arsenic in Philadelphia water? ",
            "answer": "<p>Phoenix filters utilize advanced carbon block or composite media tested against heavy metals and guarantees; your drinking water remains purified in Philadelphia. Therefore no leached arsenic from the Philadelphia water or lead from the aging pipes. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_fE9Y4D": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the best way to monitor contaminated water in Philadelphia at home?",
            "answer": "<p>The bottom line is to check the Philadelphia annual water quality report and add a Phoenix Gravity filter. This is \"final barrier\" technology that keeps out lead, bacteria, cholera, or other down-stream contamination that could creep in after the water leaves the treatment plant, through the distribution pipes. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_XzRedf": {
          "type": "text",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why is chloramine used in New York water?",
            "answer": "<p>The main reason chloramines are added to NY water is because they provide protection for the water by serving as a disinfectant while being transported through pipes to customers. Chloramines can leave a particular flavor in the water therefore, they may need to be filtered to improve their quality. </p>"
          }
        },
        "text_bVMT6E": {
          "type": "text",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is chromium 6 in New York water and should I worry?",
            "answer": "<p>Chromium (VI) is a toxic heavy metal that is found in trace amounts in NY water and is associated with industrial pollution. The greatest worry is for long-term exposure; therefore, any kind of effective filtration is very important. </p>"
          }
        }
      },
